00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
I’ve been told that we sand martins talk too much. 

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.000
That we have no sense of privacy and that our loud constant chatter is a nuisance to other creatures. 

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:21.000
But what they don’t know is: our conversations are never pointless. Our voice can save our lives.  

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:32.000
My friends Atsu and Etse were usually chosen as vigils. They were brothers, quite small for their age, who had been selected for their powerful, piercing call. 

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:48.000
They used to perch on top of a large baobab, which offered a 360° view over the forest. From there they were on the look-out, as we had many predators, and none of them scared us so much as the hawks. 

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
We lived in a constant state of emergency. 

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.000
My mom had forbidden my brothers and I to venture too far from the colony. 

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
So we stayed there, apparently lazing and chatting on the riverbanks, while really, we were just waiting for the call. 

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.000
All it took was a high-pitched shrill, a piercing siren and in a few seconds, all birds retreated inside our bunkered nest. 

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.000
It was a complex network of tunnels carved inside the clay of the banks by generations of sand martins. 

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
From the openings, we could see the hawks circling around our heads. They were just biding their time. 

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
They knew that, come summer, our food supplies would be depleted, forcing us to move further and further. 

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
They knew that the summer rain would make us slower and more vulnerable. 

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Yes, a 14g bird travelling 5000 kilometres must be mad. But staying would be even madder. 

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
On the day of departure, eighty of us joined the flock. We gathered on power lines not far from our nest. 

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Three rows of sand martins, an army of young birds ready to conquer Europe. 

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.000
For once, none of us talked. We just prayed, in silence.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
And then, as one body, we all opened our wings and took over the blue open sky. 
